# Trivy ignore file for the kubeseal-webgui-api image only.
#
# Kept separate from .trivyignore.ui because a plain Trivy ignore file
# suppresses a CVE ID globally across every image it is passed to. Each matrix
# job in .github/workflows/container-security-scan.yml passes only its own
# file.
#
# Format: CVE-ID exp:YYYY-MM-DD
# The exp: marker is real Trivy syntax (not a comment): Trivy stops honoring
# the entry once that date passes and the CVE starts failing the gate again,
# which forces a real re-check instead of relying on someone remembering to
# revisit it.
#
# The image is built on cgr.dev/chainguard/python (Wolfi), whose OS and Python
# packages scan clean. Entries are only allowed for findings we cannot fix by
# bumping or rebuilding something we control. Today that is only the
# official upstream `kubeseal` release binary, which we deliberately do not
# recompile ourselves: its embedded Go dependencies only change with a new
# sealed-secrets release (bumped by Renovate, see KUBESEAL_VERSION in
# Dockerfile.api).
#
# Re-check before each exp: date and drop any entry a newer kubeseal release
# has fixed:
#   trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH ghcr.io/jaydee94/kubeseal-webgui/api:snapshot

# kubeseal 0.41.2 — HIGH — golang.org/x/net v0.60.0 (x/net/html DoS), fixed in
# x/net 0.62.0; needs an upstream sealed-secrets release built against it.
CVE-2026-78661 exp:2027-01-31
